<!--
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    REVENANT HOUSE THEME — Obsidian / Gold / Turquoise
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    ⚠ THIS IS WARDEN'S VARIANT. It is NOT identical to Sidekick's copy and is
    not meant to be. Dave, 2026-08-03: Warden leans black and gold, with less
    turquoise than Sidekick — "not only be black and gold," but weighted there.

    What is IDENTICAL, and must stay identical — this is what makes the two read
    as a matched pair rather than two apps that happen to share a palette:

        · every resource KEY name
        · the obsidian ramp        #050506 · #0A0B0D · #12141A · #1A1D24
        · the gold ramp            #EDCF92 · #DFBE81 · #7A633E
        · the turquoise values     #7FD2D9 · #3EC2C7 · #239190
        · the five severity colors and the text colors

    What DIFFERS here, deliberately: turquoise no longer does structural work.
    In Sidekick it draws the progress bar, the selected row, and every input
    hover border. Here those are gold. Turquoise is kept for the places it earns
    — text selection, where gold on gold would be unreadable, and liveness
    indicators, which should read cool against a gold interface.

    So: same bones, different weighting. If you change a COLOR VALUE, change it
    in Sidekick too. If you change which color a CONTROL uses, that is a
    per-product decision and does not propagate.

    Palette was sampled from Dave's obsidian inlay wordmarks (three images,
    agreeing) rather than eyeballed. Gold #DFBE81 is unchanged from Warden's
    original App.xaml token — it landed exactly between the new inlay's
    highlight and mid tones. Turquoise replaces Warden's old GoldDim #2A4A62
    as the cool accent.

    Font resource keys (GhostFont / GhostFont2) and the pack:// form match
    Warden's App.xaml exactly, so both apps resolve them the same way. Both
    apps must carry the TTFs at Assets\ as <Resource> items.

    IF YOU CHANGE A COLOR HERE, CHANGE IT IN THE OTHER APP TOO. This file is
    the design contract between the pair.
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- ══ FONTS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- Family names verified against the TTF name tables (nameID 1), not
         guessed from filenames: "Ghost Theory" and "Ghost Theory 2". -->
    <!-- TitleFont  = Street Reich   — window title, section headers, wordmark
         BodyFont   = Sell Your Soul — regular UI text
         BodyAltFont= Ghost Theory   — secondary body text
         CodeFont   = Consolas       — file paths and rule IDs only, where
                                       character-level legibility is the job -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="TitleFont">pack://application:,,,/Assets/#Street Reich</FontFamily>
    <FontFamily x:Key="BodyFont">pack://application:,,,/Assets/#Sell Your Soul</FontFamily>
    <FontFamily x:Key="BodyAltFont">pack://application:,,,/Assets/#Ghost Theory</FontFamily>
    <FontFamily x:Key="CodeFont">Consolas</FontFamily>

    <!-- ⚠⚠ SELL YOUR SOUL HAS NO NUMBERS AND NO PUNCTUATION. Confirmed by Dave,
         2026-07-30. It is a letterforms-only display face: digits, apostrophes,
         periods, commas and parentheses are all absent, and WPF renders them as
         nothing at all rather than falling back to another font.

         What that looked like in the shipped window before it was caught: the
         status line read "Scan complete    finding s", the roast verdict read
         "This isn t a security audit", and SCORE showed no number.

         SO: Sell Your Soul is for ALL-CAPS, LETTERS-ONLY LABELS ONLY. Anything
         that can contain a digit or a mark of punctuation — body copy, the status
         line, the verdict, counts, scores, timings — uses TextFont below.

         Before putting Sell Your Soul on anything new, read the string. If it has
         so much as a period in it, that character disappears silently.

         Coverage measured 2026-07-30 by rendering each glyph and checking whether
         the geometry is empty. The font's own character table is NOT reliable for
         this — it claimed Sell Your Soul had periods, and it claimed Ghost Theory
         did not. Both were wrong. Render it and look at the bounds.

             Ghost Theory      draws everything probed: 0-9 . , : / ( ) ' - %
             Sell Your Soul    draws NONE of: 0 1 5 9 : . , / ( ) ' - %
             Street Reich      draws none of: ( ) ' - %   (digits and . , : / ok)

         Street Reich is only on REVENANT SYSTEMS LLC, which is letters. Fine. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="TextFont">pack://application:,,,/Assets/#Ghost Theory</FontFamily>

    <!-- Back-compat alias so nothing breaks if an old key is still referenced. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="UiFont">pack://application:,,,/Assets/#Sell Your Soul</FontFamily>
    <FontFamily x:Key="GhostFont">pack://application:,,,/Assets/#Ghost Theory</FontFamily>

    <!-- ══ OBSIDIAN — the base stone ══════════════════════════════════ -->
    <Color x:Key="ObsidianBlackColor">#050506</Color>
    <Color x:Key="ObsidianDeepColor">#0A0B0D</Color>
    <Color x:Key="ObsidianSurfaceColor">#12141A</Color>
    <Color x:Key="ObsidianRaisedColor">#1A1D24</Color>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgApp"     Color="{StaticResource ObsidianBlackColor}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgDeep"    Color="{StaticResource ObsidianDeepColor}"/>

    <!-- Panels carry real alpha so the circuit texture reads through them.
         This translucency IS the look — flattening these to opaque hex is
         what makes the app look like a generic dark theme. -->
    <!-- Panels: near-black at 92%. Was #CC12141A — 80%, and a blue-grey rather
         than a black. Dave, 2026-07-30, after trying textured card backs and
         pulling them: "let's try less transparent black." Text sits on these, and
         the background was reading through hard enough to fight it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgSurface"   Color="#EB08090C"/>  <!-- 92% — panels, cards -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgPanel"     Color="#B50A0B0D"/>  <!-- 71% — deepest wells -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgRaised"    Color="#D91A1D24"/>  <!-- 85% — raised cards -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgStatusBar" Color="#DD0E1016"/>  <!-- 87% — status strip -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BgInput"     Color="#CC080A0E"/>  <!-- text inputs -->

    <!-- ══ GOLD — the inlay veining ═══════════════════════════════════ -->
    <Color x:Key="GoldBrightColor">#EDCF92</Color>
    <Color x:Key="GoldColor">#DFBE81</Color>
    <Color x:Key="GoldDeepColor">#7A633E</Color>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Gold"       Color="{StaticResource GoldColor}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="GoldBright" Color="{StaticResource GoldBrightColor}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="GoldDeep"   Color="{StaticResource GoldDeepColor}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="GoldFaint"  Color="#33DFBE81"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="GoldGlow"   Color="#1FDFBE81"/>

    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="GoldGradient" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1">
        <GradientStop Color="{StaticResource GoldBrightColor}" Offset="0.0"/>
        <GradientStop Color="{StaticResource GoldColor}"       Offset="0.55"/>
        <GradientStop Color="{StaticResource GoldDeepColor}"   Offset="1.0"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>

    <!-- Hairline gold rule, for band edges -->
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="GoldRule" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="1,0">
        <GradientStop Color="#00DFBE81" Offset="0.0"/>
        <GradientStop Color="{StaticResource GoldColor}" Offset="0.5"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#00DFBE81" Offset="1.0"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>

    <!-- ══ TURQUOISE — the stone inlay ═══════════════════════════════ -->
    <Color x:Key="TurquoiseBrightColor">#7FD2D9</Color>
    <Color x:Key="TurquoiseColor">#3EC2C7</Color>
    <Color x:Key="TurquoiseDeepColor">#239190</Color>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Turquoise"       Color="{StaticResource TurquoiseColor}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TurquoiseBright" Color="{StaticResource TurquoiseBrightColor}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TurquoiseDeep"   Color="{StaticResource TurquoiseDeepColor}"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TurquoiseFaint"  Color="#333EC2C7"/>

    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="TurquoiseGradient" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1">
        <GradientStop Color="{StaticResource TurquoiseBrightColor}" Offset="0.0"/>
        <GradientStop Color="{StaticResource TurquoiseColor}"       Offset="0.55"/>
        <GradientStop Color="{StaticResource TurquoiseDeepColor}"   Offset="1.0"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>

    <!-- ══ BORDERS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderColor"     Color="#2D3748"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderSubtle"    Color="#1A2A3A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderGold"      Color="#59DFBE81"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderTurquoise" Color="#593EC2C7"/>

    <!-- ══ TEXT ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextPrimary" Color="#CDD4DE"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextCode"    Color="#A8B5C8"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextMuted"   Color="#5B6B7A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextOnGold"  Color="#050506"/>

    <!-- ══ SEVERITY / GRADE ═══════════════════════════════════════════
         Set by Dave 2026-07-30 from a colour sketch drawn straight over a
         screenshot of the running app:

             Info      light blue    #7FA6C9
             Low       yellow        #F0DE1B
             Medium    orange        #F59A20
             High      red           #DA2B26
             Critical  deep crimson  #8C1436

         A pure heat ramp — yellow, orange, red — and then Critical goes DOWN into
         crimson rather than further up. Critical is the only value that gets
         darker instead of hotter, which is what tells it apart from High at a
         glance rather than by hue alone.

         Earlier passes ran teal Low and purple Critical. Both were replaced by
         the sketch above. Do not reconstruct them.

         Same values live in SeverityBrushes.cs and HtmlReporter.Css(), so a
         finding reads identically on screen and in an exported report. Three
         places, one palette — change all three.

         ⚠ Critical #8C1436 is dark on purpose and measures roughly 2:1 against
         obsidian. Fine as a fill, a border or a chip, which is all it is used for.
         Do not put small text in it on the app background.                    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevCritical" Color="#8C1436"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevHigh"     Color="#DA2B26"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevMedium"   Color="#F59A20"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevLow"      Color="#F0DE1B"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevInfo"     Color="#7FA6C9"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Jade"        Color="#3AD5A1"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevCriticalWash" Color="#268C1436"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevHighWash"     Color="#26DA2B26"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevMediumWash"   Color="#26F59A20"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevLowWash"      Color="#26F0DE1B"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevInfoWash"     Color="#267FA6C9"/>

    <!-- Brighter fills for the selected/active state of a severity card. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevCriticalActive" Color="#598C1436"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevHighActive"     Color="#59DA2B26"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevMediumActive"   Color="#59F59A20"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevLowActive"      Color="#59F0DE1B"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SevInfoActive"     Color="#597FA6C9"/>

    <!-- ══ TYPE STYLES ════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <Style x:Key="Wordmark" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource GhostFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource GoldGradient}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="40"/>
        <Setter Property="TextOptions.TextFormattingMode" Value="Ideal"/>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="SubWordmark" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource GhostFont2}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Turquoise}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="17"/>
        <Setter Property="TextOptions.TextFormattingMode" Value="Ideal"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ⚠ These are Sell Your Soul, NOT Street Reich, and that is deliberate.
         Street Reich is reserved for exactly two pieces of text in the whole app:
         SECURITY AUDIT in the header band, and REVENANT SYSTEMS in the footer.
         Both set it inline. Dave, 2026-07-30, after seeing it applied to every
         section label: "these shouldn't be Street Reich, only these two."
         Do not promote it back into these shared styles.

         Sizes stay up from the original 9 and 10 — that part was never the
         problem with the font, 9px was just too small. Eyebrow was GoldDeep
         #7A633E, about 3.6:1 on obsidian, under the line for text this size.
         Gold measures about 11:1. -->
    <Style x:Key="Eyebrow" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource UiFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Gold}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13"/>
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="SectionLabel" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource UiFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Gold}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="16"/>
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,6"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- Base text. Bold by default — Sell Your Soul is a thin display face and at
         UI sizes its strokes drop out; the letters literally lose pieces. WPF has
         no real bold for it (the TTF ships one face) so this is a synthesized
         bold, which is exactly what it is for.

         ⚠ NO FontSize SETTER HERE, DELIBERATELY. There used to be one, at 12, and
         it was silently overriding every control in the app: when a Button or
         CheckBox has a plain string as content, WPF generates a TextBlock for it,
         this implicit style lands on that TextBlock, and a style setter outranks
         the value inherited from the control. So setting FontSize on a Button did
         nothing at all. Without the setter, text inherits from its container and
         control font sizes work the way you'd expect. -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBlock">
        <!-- ⚠ POLICY INVERTED 2026-08-09, at Dave's call.

             This used to default to UiFont (Sell Your Soul) and expect every
             string containing a digit or a punctuation mark to opt OUT to
             TextFont. That is backwards, because the failure is SILENT: the
             glyph simply is not drawn, nothing errors, and nobody notices.
             What it actually cost — the Engine Settings temperature readout is
             a bare number like "0.7" and had been rendering as empty space; six
             labels had quietly lost their trailing colons; "Context Limit
             (Tokens):" had lost its parentheses too.

             TextFont (Ghost Theory) draws everything probed — 0-9 . , : / ( ) ' - %
             — so it is the default now. Sell Your Soul is OPT-IN, via the
             Eyebrow and SectionLabel styles above, where the string is known to
             be ALL-CAPS letters only. Reach for it deliberately; never inherit it. -->
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextPrimary}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ BUTTON ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- 14, not 12. Sell Your Soul is a thin display face and at 12 its stems
         start dropping out on control labels — "Browse", "Roast", "Student mode"
         lose pieces of letters. SCAN reads fine at the same size only because
         PrimaryButton is bold on a gold fill. PrimaryButton inherits this size. -->
    <!-- TextFont, not UiFont — same inversion as TextBlock above. Button content
         is where this bit hardest: the window controls are Buttons whose entire
         content is a symbol (— ⛶ ✕), and the file already records Sell Your Soul
         eating the arrow off "◄ WS". A face with no punctuation has no business
         being the default for control labels. -->
    <Style TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Gold}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14"/>
        <!-- ⚠ 4,2 here, NOT Sidekick's 14,6. Warden's buttons are small chrome
             buttons with hard-coded Width and Height — the window controls are
             26px square, and 14px of horizontal padding per side wants 28px on
             its own, so their glyphs clipped to nothing the moment this style
             started applying. Eleven buttons carry a fixed Width in
             MainWindow.xaml; six of them are too narrow for Sidekick's padding.

             Sidekick's buttons are roomy text buttons that size to content, so
             14,6 is right there and wrong here. A button that wants breathing
             room sets its own Padding. -->
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="4,2"/>
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="3,0"/>
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
        <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <Border x:Name="bd"
                            Background="{StaticResource BgRaised}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource BorderGold}"
                            BorderThickness="1"
                            CornerRadius="2"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                            <!-- ⚠ WITHOUT THIS, A BUTTON'S OWN FONT SETTINGS ARE IGNORED.
                                 When Content is a plain string, WPF generates a TextBlock
                                 for it, and the app-level implicit TextBlock style lands on
                                 that TextBlock — a style setter outranks a value inherited
                                 from the Button. So FontFamily="Consolas" on a Button did
                                 nothing and the content silently rendered in Sell Your Soul.

                                 That ate the arrow off "◄ WS" (Sell Your Soul has no ◄) and
                                 turned "Set Root" into small caps. Styles declared here are
                                 closer in scope than the app dictionary, so these win. -->
                            <ContentPresenter.Resources>
                                <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type TextBlock}}">
                                    <Setter Property="FontFamily"
                                            Value="{Binding FontFamily, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type Button}}}"/>
                                    <Setter Property="FontSize"
                                            Value="{Binding FontSize, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type Button}}}"/>
                                    <Setter Property="FontWeight"
                                            Value="{Binding FontWeight, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type Button}}}"/>
                                </Style>
                            </ContentPresenter.Resources>
                        </ContentPresenter>
                    </Border>
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource GoldGlow}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Gold}"/>
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource GoldBright}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                        <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource GoldFaint}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                        <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource BorderSubtle}"/>
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMuted}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- Primary action: solid gold, obsidian text -->
    <Style x:Key="PrimaryButton" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnGold}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="26,7"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <Border x:Name="bd"
                            Background="{StaticResource GoldGradient}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource GoldBright}"
                            BorderThickness="1"
                            CornerRadius="2"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    </Border>
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource GoldBright}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                        <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource GoldDeep}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                        <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource BgRaised}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource BorderSubtle}"/>
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMuted}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ TEXTBOX ════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBox">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource BgInput}"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextPrimary}"/>
        <Setter Property="CaretBrush" Value="{StaticResource Gold}"/>
        <Setter Property="SelectionBrush" Value="{StaticResource Turquoise}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource CodeFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13"/>
        <!-- TextBox draws its own text rather than going through a TextBlock, so
             it does not pick up the base style's weight. Set it here. Consolas
             has a real bold face, unlike the display fonts. -->
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,6"/>
        <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource BorderColor}"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="TextBox">
                    <Border x:Name="bd" Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                            BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                            BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                            CornerRadius="2">
                        <ScrollViewer x:Name="PART_ContentHost"
                                      Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                      VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    </Border>
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource BorderGold}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                        <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Gold}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ CHECKBOX ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- TextFont, same inversion. -->
    <Style TargetType="CheckBox">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextCode}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14"/>
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="CheckBox">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Background="Transparent">
                        <Border x:Name="box" Width="14" Height="14"
                                Background="{StaticResource BgInput}"
                                BorderBrush="{StaticResource BorderColor}"
                                BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="2"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center">
                            <Path x:Name="check" Stretch="Uniform" Margin="2"
                                  Data="M 0,5 L 3.5,8.5 L 9,1"
                                  Stroke="{StaticResource Gold}" StrokeThickness="2"
                                  Visibility="Collapsed"/>
                        </Border>
                        <ContentPresenter Margin="7,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    </StackPanel>
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="check" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="box" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Gold}"/>
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextPrimary}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="box" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource BorderGold}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ COMBOBOX ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- ⚠ TextFont (Ghost Theory), NOT UiFont (Sell Your Soul). A ComboBox shows
         DATA, and data has digits in it. The model list is the proof: an Ollama
         tag like "qwen2.5-coder:7b" rendered as "qwencodercb" because Sell Your
         Soul silently draws nothing for 0-9 . : and -. See the font warning at
         the top of this file. Changed 2026-08-09 after Dave hit it live.
         Sidekick's copy of this theme has the same two lines (411 and 433) and
         has NOT been changed. -->
    <Style x:Key="DarkComboBoxItemStyle" TargetType="ComboBoxItem">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextPrimary}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,5"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="ComboBoxItem">
                    <Border x:Name="bd" Background="Transparent" Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter/>
                    </Border>
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsHighlighted" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource GoldFaint}"/>
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource GoldBright}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- TextFont here too, for the selected-item text. Both selectors sit one
         above the other, so they must share a face — Provider in Sell Your Soul
         and Model in Ghost Theory would read as a mistake even though only the
         model list actually needs the digits. -->
    <Style TargetType="ComboBox">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextPrimary}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14"/>
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="30"/>
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
        <Setter Property="ItemContainerStyle" Value="{StaticResource DarkComboBoxItemStyle}"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="ComboBox">
                    <Grid>
                        <ToggleButton x:Name="tb" Focusable="False" ClickMode="Press"
                                      IsChecked="{Binding IsDropDownOpen, Mode=TwoWay, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}">
                            <ToggleButton.Template>
                                <ControlTemplate TargetType="ToggleButton">
                                    <Border x:Name="bd" Background="{StaticResource BgInput}"
                                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource BorderColor}"
                                            BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="2">
                                        <Path HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                              Margin="0,0,8,0" Data="M 0,0 L 8,0 L 4,5 Z"
                                              Fill="{StaticResource Gold}"/>
                                    </Border>
                                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                            <Setter TargetName="bd" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Gold}"/>
                                        </Trigger>
                                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                                </ControlTemplate>
                            </ToggleButton.Template>
                        </ToggleButton>
                        <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding SelectionBoxItem}"
                                          Margin="9,0,24,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                          IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                        <Popup AllowsTransparency="True" Placement="Bottom" Focusable="False"
                               IsOpen="{TemplateBinding IsDropDownOpen}"
                               PopupAnimation="Fade">
                            <Border Background="#F20A0B0D"
                                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource BorderGold}"
                                    BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="2"
                                    MinWidth="{TemplateBinding ActualWidth}"
                                    MaxHeight="{TemplateBinding MaxDropDownHeight}">
                                <ScrollViewer>
                                    <ItemsPresenter/>
                                </ScrollViewer>
                            </Border>
                        </Popup>
                    </Grid>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ SLIDER ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Added for Warden 2026-08-04. There was no Slider style, so TempSlider
         in Engine Settings fell back to WPF's stock template — light chrome
         built for a light UI, drawing near-white tick marks across an obsidian
         panel. That row of vertical bars at the bottom of the window was its
         eleven ticks (0.0 to 1.0 at 0.1), not a broken control.

         Track is a thin gold rule, thumb is a small gold block. Ticks are
         suppressed; IsSnapToTickEnabled still works without drawing them. -->
    <Style TargetType="Thumb" x:Key="SliderThumb">
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Thumb">
                    <Border x:Name="th" Width="10" Height="18" CornerRadius="2"
                            Background="{StaticResource GoldGradient}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource GoldBright}"
                            BorderThickness="1"/>
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="th" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource GoldBright}"/>
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="Slider">
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="22"/>
        <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Slider">
                    <Grid VerticalAlignment="Center">
                        <Border Height="3" CornerRadius="2"
                                Background="{StaticResource BgInput}"
                                BorderBrush="{StaticResource BorderGold}"
                                BorderThickness="1"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        <Track x:Name="PART_Track">
                            <Track.DecreaseRepeatButton>
                                <RepeatButton Command="Slider.DecreaseLarge" Focusable="False">
                                    <RepeatButton.Template>
                                        <ControlTemplate TargetType="RepeatButton">
                                            <Border Height="3" CornerRadius="2" Margin="0,0,0,0"
                                                    Background="{StaticResource Gold}"
                                                    VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                        </ControlTemplate>
                                    </RepeatButton.Template>
                                </RepeatButton>
                            </Track.DecreaseRepeatButton>
                            <Track.Thumb>
                                <Thumb Style="{StaticResource SliderThumb}"/>
                            </Track.Thumb>
                            <Track.IncreaseRepeatButton>
                                <RepeatButton Command="Slider.IncreaseLarge" Focusable="False">
                                    <RepeatButton.Template>
                                        <ControlTemplate TargetType="RepeatButton">
                                            <Border Background="Transparent"/>
                                        </ControlTemplate>
                                    </RepeatButton.Template>
                                </RepeatButton>
                            </Track.IncreaseRepeatButton>
                        </Track>
                    </Grid>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ PROGRESSBAR ════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <Style TargetType="ProgressBar">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource BgInput}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource BorderSubtle}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
        <!-- Gold, not turquoise. Sidekick's progress bar is the single largest
             turquoise element on its screen; here the bar is gold and turquoise
             stays out of the chrome. -->
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource GoldGradient}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ DATAGRID ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!-- TextFont, same inversion. Column headers can carry punctuation
         ("File:Line" being the obvious one). -->
    <Style TargetType="DataGridColumnHeader">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="#CC0E1016"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Gold}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFont}"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12"/>
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,8"/>
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource BorderSubtle}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,0,1,1"/>
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="DataGridRow">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextPrimary}"/>
        <Style.Triggers>
            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource GoldGlow}"/>
            </Trigger>
            <!-- Gold selection, not turquoise. Same reasoning as the progress bar. -->
            <Trigger Property="IsSelected" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource GoldFaint}"/>
            </Trigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="DataGridCell">
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,7"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextPrimary}"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="DataGridCell">
                    <Border Background="Transparent" Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    </Border>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="DataGrid">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource BgPanel}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource BorderColor}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
        <Setter Property="RowBackground" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="AlternatingRowBackground" Value="#14FFFFFF"/>
        <Setter Property="HorizontalGridLinesBrush" Value="{StaticResource BorderSubtle}"/>
        <Setter Property="VerticalGridLinesBrush" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="RowHeight" Value="30"/>
        <Setter Property="ColumnHeaderHeight" Value="30"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ══ TOOLTIP ════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <Style TargetType="ToolTip">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="#F20A0B0D"/>
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextPrimary}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource BorderGold}"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,5"/>
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11"/>
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
